A||||5||spiz||DOHC  intermediate shaft removal||||1||
Z||000000||spiz||04-29-2001||01:34 PM||||For those who have rebuilt a dohc. How do you get the gear off the intermediate shaft? tia<BR>spiz||||reg||1
Z||000001||87GTZ34||04-29-2001||09:53 PM||||Yeah! That is an excellent question.  Usually I'm pretty resourceful and don't cave-in to buying designer's 'special' tools but...  I had to on this one.  The book says to buy J8433 and J38611. If I had a welder and torches in my garage, I could have made the tool.  I need to buy a auto shop or something...<BR>I purchased the 38611 and used my puller in place of the J8433.  I think the price was around $100 USD ouch!  I couldn't think of any other way without deystroying something and I don't like to butcher mechanical things.  Glad to see someone else working on the DOHC, I have a '96.  Can you give details about your project or is it a secret?  (some have secret DOHC projects in progress...)<P>Here's the tool link <A HREF="http://www.spxkentmoore.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.spxkentmoore.com/</A> <P>good luck!  email me if you want to discuss anything with regard to the DOHC<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 04-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||3400Fiero||04-29-2001||09:53 PM||||Are you referring to the timing belt sprocket or the timing chain sprocket?  ie the one on the outside or on the inside?  From what I remember, the one on the outside comes off pretty easily, but the one on the inside will require a gear puller.  Should be able to rent one at your local auto parts store.  Good luck!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>1988 Fiero 3400DOHC||||reg||5
Z||000003||spiz||04-30-2001||08:46 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 87GTZ34:<BR><B>  Glad to see someone else working on the DOHC, I have a '96.  Can you give details about your project or is it a secret?  (some have secret DOHC projects in progress...)<P>Here's the tool link <A HREF="http://www.spxkentmoore.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.spxkentmoore.com/</A> <P>good luck!  email me if you want to discuss anything with regard to the DOHC<P>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 04-29-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR> Thanks for the info. I dont think I will buy the tool,too expensive. No secrects here I need all the help I can get. Engine is from a 92 lumina.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by spiz (edited 04-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||spiz||04-30-2001||08:58 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 3400Fiero:<BR><B>Are you referring to the timing belt sprocket or the timing chain sprocket?<BR> <BR> 3400 I am talking about the timing belt(outside)sprocket. No way to get a puller on it. Can't pry it off. It is on very tight.<BR> On a different note. At one time you said you might be creating a web site to document your swap. Are you still planning on doing that?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><p>[This message has been edited by spiz (edited 04-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GTZ34||04-30-2001||12:03 PM||||Spiz,  that tool is for the intermediate shaft chain sprocket.  For the cog 'sprocket', you should be able to use a universal puller from a AZ, PEPB etc. BTW, in the manual they refer to both as the intermediate shaft drive sprocket.||||reg||1
